*Abstract.* When a robot returns to a remembered object, a panel can conceal the object and its empty location equally well. We audit a decision to record removal by rendering both states from the same camera pose. The audit comprises 1,280 two-image queries from 16 shelf and tabletop layouts shared by Isaac Sim and MuJoCo. After a 6 cm camera shift and 3° yaw with the blocker parked, replacing homography with dense registration increased correct removal decisions from 67/80 to 76/80 in Isaac Sim and from 63/80 to 78/80 in MuJoCo. The same change introduced two false deletions on MuJoCo shelves and failed the prespecified improvement criterion. At 88 positions the target was fully hidden in both simulators. MuJoCo produced identical reference images, boxes and revisits across opposite states at all 88 positions. Homography abstained on every such pair. Dense geometry declared removal in both states on one pair. On the retained member of that pair, the update would erase a valid memory. Isaac Sim produced unequal images at all 88 positions, which leaves their visual answerability unresolved. Reporting these pairs alongside clear removals measures both useful updates and commitments where the images cannot distinguish the physical states.
